  "worked_solution": "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\u22ef,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x$ and $a=2\\pi .$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x \\\\ f(2\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2\\pi )=1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(2\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2\\pi )=0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(2\\pi ) & =-\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2\\pi )=-1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(2\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2\\pi )=0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(2\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2\\pi )=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & =(1)+(0)(x-2\\pi )+\\frac{(-1)}{2!}(x-2\\pi )^{2}+\\frac{(0)}{3!}(x-2\\pi )^{3}+\\frac{(1)}{4!}(x-2\\pi )^{4}+\u22ef \\\\  & =1-\\frac{(x-2\\pi )^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(x-2\\pi )^{4}}{24}+\u22ef\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=5$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(5).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(5) & \u22481-\\frac{(5-2\\pi )^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(5-2\\pi )^{4}}{24} \\\\  & \u22480.29,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.",
